 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Gaussian Probability Modeling
-This variant refers specifically to the mechanism of estimating the probability distribution for the attributes. It involves the context model outputting individual mean $\mu_i$ and variance $\sigma_i$ for each quantized attribute $\hat{\mathbf{f}}_i$ to formulate respective Gaussian distributions $\mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma_i)$ [S1, unofficial]. This approach is chosen because anchor attributes in the referenced work exhibit Gaussian-like distributions [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart by relying on parametric density estimation rather than histogram-based or non-parametric methods.
+This variant describes the mechanism of estimating the probability distribution for the attributes [S1, unofficial]. The context model outputs individual mean $\mu_i$ and variance $\sigma_i$ for each quantized attribute $\hat{\mathbf{f}}_i$ to formulate respective Gaussian distributions $\mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma_i)$ [S1, unofficial]. This approach is chosen because the anchor attributes exhibit Gaussian-like distributions [S1, unofficial]. It relies on parametric density estimation rather than histogram-based or non-parametric methods [G].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of anchors $N^{\text{at}}$ and the number of attribute types $|\mathbf{A}|$ [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the context model outputs ($\mu_i, \sigma_i$) for all anchors and attributes during the forward pass [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Directly optimizes for compression efficiency by minimizing the theoretical bit rate required to encode the attributes [S1, unofficial]. It adapts to the data distribution via the context model [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the Gaussian assumption; if the attribute distribution is multi-modal or heavy-tailed, the Gaussian model may underestimate the entropy [S1, unofficial]. Requires careful tuning of quantization steps $Q$ for different attributes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of quantization steps $Q$ for different attributes [S1, unofficial].
 *   **When to use:** When the goal is to compress neural Gaussian splats or similar volume representations by reducing the bit depth of stored attributes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When the attribute distributions are known to be non-Gaussian and a more flexible density estimator is available, or when compression is not a primary constraint.
 
